	<p>
	</p>
	  
      </section>

      <section class="topic-section" id="topological-space">
        <h2>Topological Space</h2>

	<p>
	  Let <Math tex="X"/> be a set and <Math tex="\mathcal{T} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)"/> a subset of its powerset (meaning a collection of subsets of <Math tex="X"/>).
	  We say the structure <Math tex = "(X, \mathcal{T})"/> is a <span class="emph"> topology </span> or <span class="emph"> topological space </span> if the following
	  axioms are verified:
	</p>

	<p>
	  (<Math tex="T_1"/>) <Math tex="X"/> and <Math tex="\emptyset"/> are in <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/>;
	</p>

	<p>
	  (<Math tex="T_2"/>) Any arbitrary union of sets from <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/> is still in <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/>;
	</p>

	<p>
	  (<Math tex="T_3"/>) Any finite intersection of sets from <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/> is still in <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/>.
	</p>

	<p> Elements of <Math tex="\mathcal{T}"/> are refered to as <span class = "emph">open sets</span>. </p>

	<p>
	  Now, the above definition is cool and all that... but seriously, what does any of it even mean? If this is your first introduction to topology, chance is you either know
	  next to nothing about the field, or at most you've likely seen memes or visual explanations floating around that look something akin to this:
	</p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="mug-to-donut-scene"
	  label="A visualization of how to deform a mug-like shape into a torus."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>

	<p> Or maybe this: </p>

	<MathCanvas2D
	  id="cow-to-sphere-scene"
	  label="A visualization of how to deform a cow-like shape into a sphere."
	  aspectRatio="16 / 9"
	/>
	
      </section>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

